steps.body:
A signal never trades a raw quote. It trades features: numbers that mean the same thing at the open and at the close, in any stock. Five cover most of what a quote says:

- **Mid**, `(bid + ask) / 2`: the fair price between the two sides.
- **Spread**, `ask - bid` in ticks: what crossing costs, and a filter, since wide means a thin, nervous book.
- **Microprice**, `(bid * ask_sz + ask * bid_sz) / (bid_sz + ask_sz)`: the mid leaned toward the side with *less* size, because that queue empties first and the price tends to move that way.
- **Imbalance**, `(bid_sz - ask_sz) / (bid_sz + ask_sz)`: the same pressure as one number, from -1 with all the size on the ask to 1 with all of it on the bid.
- **Z-score**, `(x - mean) / stdev` over a rolling window: how unusual the price is right now, the input to mean reversion, which bets that an unusual price comes back.

Prices arrive as integer ticks, but features are statistics, so they're doubles. Only money (fills and P&L) has to stay exact.

The z-score uses the rolling Welford window from *Statistics on a Stream: EWMA, Welford, and Correlation*: a `std::array` ring of the last `N` values, updated in O(1) by each `push`, with no allocation. Its size is fixed at compile time:

```cpp harness
// Welford's mean and variance over the last N values, in a fixed ring.
template <std::size_t N>
class RollingStats {
public:
    void push(double x) {
        if (count_ < N) {
            ++count_;
            const double d = x - mean_;
            mean_ += d / count_;
            m2_ += d * (x - mean_);
        } else {
            const double old = ring_[head_];
            const double old_mean = mean_;
            mean_ += (x - old) / N;
            m2_ += (x - old) * (x - mean_ + old - old_mean);
        }
        ring_[head_] = x;
        head_ = (head_ + 1) % N;
    }
    bool full() const { return count_ == N; }
    double mean() const { return mean_; }
    double stdev() const { return std::sqrt(std::max(m2_, 0.0) / (count_ - 1)); }

private:
    std::array<double, N> ring_{};
    std::size_t head_ = 0;
    std::size_t count_ = 0;
    double mean_ = 0.0;
    double m2_ = 0.0;
};
{{code}}
```

```cpp
std::cout << sizeof(RollingStats<4>) << ' ' << sizeof(RollingStats<1024>) << '\n';  // 64 8224
```

Four doubles of history plus the head, the count, the mean, and `m2` come to 64 bytes, the size of one cache line.

